<header><p class="st-kicker">other · Korea and Japan</p><h1 class="st-title">Choshu Domain</h1><p class="st-meta">1600 CE – 1871 CE · capital Hagi, later Yamaguchi</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The Mori domain at the western tip of Honshu that led the radical movement against the shogunate and supplied many leaders of Meiji Japan.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>After the Mori led the losing side at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-sekigahara">Sekigahara</a> in 1600, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-ieyasu">Tokugawa Ieyasu</a> reduced their lands to the provinces of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suo-province">Suo</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nagato-province">Nagato</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mori-terumoto">Mori Terumoto's</a> son Hidenari ruled from Hagi castle, built from 1604, and the family's grievance against the Tokugawa was said to be kept alive for generations.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/choshu-domain">Choshu</a> developed paper, salt and wax and reformed its finances under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/murata-seifu">Murata Seifu</a> in the 1840s. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yoshida-shoin">Yoshida Shoin's</a> private school at Hagi trained young <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samurai">samurai</a> who embraced the slogan revere the emperor, expel the barbarians. In 1863 Choshu fired on foreign ships at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shimonoseki">Shimonoseki</a>, moved its seat to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yamaguchi-city">Yamaguchi</a> and was bombarded by Western fleets in 1864.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Defeated in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyoto">Kyoto</a> in 1864, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/choshu-domain">Choshu</a> was rebuilt by radicals around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/takasugi-shinsaku">Takasugi Shinsaku's</a> mixed militia, beat the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shogun">shogunate</a>'s army in 1866 and allied with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/satsuma-domain">Satsuma</a>. Its men, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kido-takayoshi">Kido Takayoshi</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ito-hirobumi">Ito Hirobumi</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yamagata-aritomo">Yamagata Aritomo</a>, led <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/empire-of-japan">Meiji Japan</a>; it became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yamaguchi-prefecture">Yamaguchi Prefecture</a> in 1871.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/choshu-domain.jpg" alt="Choshu Domain" width="320" height="212"><figcaption>Image: by Reggaeman · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hagi_Castle_The_Keep_Base.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1600 CE – 1871 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>other</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Hagi, later Yamaguchi</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Mori Hidenari, Mori Takachika</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Buddhism, Shinto</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Japanese</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/japan">Japan</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Hagi Domain, Choshu han, Nagato Domain</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1736&amp;focus=choshu-domain" aria-label="Choshu Domain on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="8021 931 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Choshu Domain lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M8341 1187L8530 1145M8341 1187L8529 1145"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M8530 1145h0M8529 1145h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M8341 1187h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M8341 1187h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Hagi. <a href="/atlas?year=1736&amp;focus=choshu-domain">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
